1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,134
Sweetheart, biscuits and sausage gravy is the

2
00:00:03,134 --> 00:00:05,503
breakfast that built this country.

3
00:00:05,683 --> 00:00:08,388
Tall, flaky buttermilk biscuits,

4
00:00:08,388 --> 00:00:11,432
split open and smothered in a thick,

5
00:00:11,432 --> 00:00:14,646
peppery, creamy gravy full of sausage.

6
00:00:14,826 --> 00:00:17,038
But most of what I'm served in

7
00:00:17,038 --> 00:00:19,914
restaurants is a flat hockey puck under

8
00:00:19,914 --> 00:00:22,568
a gluey, gray paste that tastes like

9
00:00:22,568 --> 00:00:23,011
flour.

10
00:00:23,191 --> 00:00:26,499
And it's always the same five mistakes.

11
00:00:26,679 --> 00:00:28,967
Every one is easy to fix.

12
00:00:29,317 --> 00:00:31,983
So tonight I'm giving you the biscuits

13
00:00:31,983 --> 00:00:34,720
and gravy my mother made every Saturday

14
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:36,264
morning on Elm Street,

15
00:00:36,264 --> 00:00:37,176
step by step.

16
00:00:37,356 --> 00:00:38,581
And stay with me,

17
00:00:38,581 --> 00:00:41,101
because the last secret is what she

18
00:00:41,101 --> 00:00:43,405
did with a plain drinking glass,

19
00:00:43,405 --> 00:00:45,782
and it's why her biscuits rose up

20
00:00:45,782 --> 00:00:48,230
so tall that my father called them

21
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:49,094
skyscrapers.

22
00:00:49,444 --> 00:00:52,210
Saturday was the only morning my father

23
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:54,338
didn't have to be at the plant

24
00:00:54,338 --> 00:00:55,331
by six, honey.

25
00:00:55,511 --> 00:00:57,902
So Saturday was biscuit day.

26
00:00:58,082 --> 00:01:01,099
My mother was up at five-thirty anyway,

27
00:01:01,099 --> 00:01:02,105
out of habit,

28
00:01:02,105 --> 00:01:04,193
and by the time Danny and I

29
00:01:04,193 --> 00:01:06,591
came downstairs in our pajamas,

30
00:01:06,591 --> 00:01:09,685
the whole house smelled like sausage and

31
00:01:09,685 --> 00:01:10,226
butter.

32
00:01:10,406 --> 00:01:12,448
My father would be at the table

33
00:01:12,448 --> 00:01:14,688
with his coffee and the newspaper,

34
00:01:14,688 --> 00:01:16,664
already on his second biscuit.

35
00:01:17,015 --> 00:01:20,020
Biscuits and gravy comes from the South

36
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:21,715
and Appalachia, honey,

37
00:01:21,715 --> 00:01:25,028
back in the seventeen hundreds and eighteen

38
00:01:25,028 --> 00:01:25,722
hundreds.

39
00:01:25,902 --> 00:01:28,815
Working families needed a breakfast that was

40
00:01:28,815 --> 00:01:31,661
cheap, filling, and would hold them through

41
00:01:31,661 --> 00:01:33,448
a long day in the fields or

42
00:01:33,448 --> 00:01:34,110
the mines.

43
00:01:34,290 --> 00:01:37,413
Flour, lard, and a little pork were

44
00:01:37,413 --> 00:01:39,375
all most families had.

45
00:01:39,555 --> 00:01:42,226
The gravy was called sawmill gravy,

46
00:01:42,226 --> 00:01:44,743
because the loggers ate it at the

47
00:01:44,743 --> 00:01:45,430
sawmills.

48
00:01:45,610 --> 00:01:47,703
In a minute I'll tell you how

49
00:01:47,703 --> 00:01:48,931
it got that name.

50
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:51,663
Some people say it got the name

51
00:01:51,663 --> 00:01:54,200
because it was so thick and lumpy

52
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:55,968
it looked like sawdust.

53
00:01:56,148 --> 00:01:58,586
Others say the cooks at the logging

54
00:01:58,586 --> 00:02:00,885
camps made it in huge kettles for

55
00:02:00,885 --> 00:02:02,348
the men at the mills.

56
00:02:02,528 --> 00:02:05,035
My father said it was because after

57
00:02:05,035 --> 00:02:06,038
a plate of it,

58
00:02:06,038 --> 00:02:07,973
you could saw wood all day.

59
00:02:08,153 --> 00:02:09,549
Whatever the reason,

60
00:02:09,549 --> 00:02:12,132
honey, it kept half the country going

61
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:13,667
for two hundred years.

62
00:02:13,847 --> 00:02:16,442
And it only costs about two dollars

63
00:02:16,442 --> 00:02:17,702
to feed a family.

64
00:02:18,052 --> 00:02:19,365
For the biscuits,

65
00:02:19,365 --> 00:02:22,380
you need two cups of all-purpose flour,

66
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:24,853
one tablespoon of baking powder,

67
00:02:24,853 --> 00:02:27,249
a half teaspoon of baking soda,

68
00:02:27,249 --> 00:02:28,872
one teaspoon of salt,

69
00:02:28,872 --> 00:02:31,191
one stick of very cold butter,

70
00:02:31,191 --> 00:02:33,510
and three quarters of a cup of

71
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:34,747
cold buttermilk.

72
00:02:34,927 --> 00:02:35,763
That's it.

73
00:02:35,943 --> 00:02:37,475
Six ingredients.

74
00:02:37,655 --> 00:02:39,304
My mother used lard,

75
00:02:39,304 --> 00:02:42,603
but butter tastes better and it's easier

76
00:02:42,603 --> 00:02:43,263
to find.

77
00:02:43,443 --> 00:02:45,510
Some people use half and half,

78
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:47,715
half butter and half shortening.

79
00:02:48,066 --> 00:02:49,256
For the gravy,

80
00:02:49,256 --> 00:02:51,892
one pound of breakfast sausage,

81
00:02:51,892 --> 00:02:54,019
the plain kind in a roll,

82
00:02:54,019 --> 00:02:54,869
not links.

83
00:02:55,049 --> 00:02:57,127
A quarter cup of flour.

84
00:02:57,307 --> 00:02:59,157
Two and a half to three cups

85
00:02:59,157 --> 00:03:00,082
of whole milk.

86
00:03:00,262 --> 00:03:02,178
A half teaspoon of salt,

87
00:03:02,178 --> 00:03:05,053
and a full teaspoon of black pepper,

88
00:03:05,053 --> 00:03:07,449
freshly ground if you have it.

89
00:03:07,629 --> 00:03:09,911
And a little pinch of something at

90
00:03:09,911 --> 00:03:12,261
the end, which I'll tell you about.

91
00:03:12,441 --> 00:03:13,521
That's all.

92
00:03:13,701 --> 00:03:16,970
Biscuits and gravy is about technique,

93
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:18,948
honey, not ingredients.

94
00:03:19,299 --> 00:03:20,969
About the buttermilk,

95
00:03:20,969 --> 00:03:21,446
honey.

96
00:03:21,626 --> 00:03:25,242
Real buttermilk makes the biscuits tender and

97
00:03:25,242 --> 00:03:26,448
a little tangy,

98
00:03:26,448 --> 00:03:28,778
and the acid makes them rise.

99
00:03:28,958 --> 00:03:30,488
If you don't have any,

100
00:03:30,488 --> 00:03:32,852
put a tablespoon of lemon juice or

101
00:03:32,852 --> 00:03:35,147
white vinegar in a measuring cup,

102
00:03:35,147 --> 00:03:37,233
fill it to three quarters of a

103
00:03:37,233 --> 00:03:38,555
cup with cold milk,

104
00:03:38,555 --> 00:03:40,849
and let it sit five minutes until

105
00:03:40,849 --> 00:03:42,310
it thickens a little.

106
00:03:42,490 --> 00:03:45,083
That's what my mother did during the

107
00:03:45,083 --> 00:03:47,749
war, when buttermilk was hard to get.

108
00:03:48,099 --> 00:03:50,850
Mistake number one is warm butter.

109
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:52,943
If your butter is soft,

110
00:03:52,943 --> 00:03:54,940
it melts into the flour,

111
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:56,354
and you get flat,

112
00:03:56,354 --> 00:03:57,601
dense biscuits.

113
00:03:57,781 --> 00:04:00,302
The butter has to be ice cold,

114
00:04:00,302 --> 00:04:02,739
so it stays in little pieces.

115
00:04:02,919 --> 00:04:03,866
In the oven,

116
00:04:03,866 --> 00:04:06,470
those pieces melt and make steam,

117
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:08,917
and the steam is what makes the

118
00:04:08,917 --> 00:04:09,943
flaky layers.

119
00:04:10,123 --> 00:04:12,156
Put your stick of butter in the

120
00:04:12,156 --> 00:04:13,927
freezer for twenty minutes,

121
00:04:13,927 --> 00:04:16,025
then grate it on a cheese grater

122
00:04:16,025 --> 00:04:17,402
right into the flour.

123
00:04:17,753 --> 00:04:20,725
If Grandma's recipe is helping you,

124
00:04:20,725 --> 00:04:23,018
tap the like button for me,

125
00:04:23,018 --> 00:04:23,952
sweetheart.

126
00:04:24,132 --> 00:04:27,580
It helps more people find these videos.

127
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:31,023
Mistake number two is overworking the dough.

128
00:04:31,203 --> 00:04:33,707
Stir in the buttermilk with a fork,

129
00:04:33,707 --> 00:04:36,068
just until it comes together in a

130
00:04:36,068 --> 00:04:36,927
shaggy mess.

131
00:04:37,107 --> 00:04:38,906
Don't knead it like bread.

132
00:04:39,086 --> 00:04:42,337
Every extra stir makes the biscuits tougher.

133
00:04:42,517 --> 00:04:43,596
My mother said,

134
00:04:43,596 --> 00:04:46,544
treat biscuit dough like a sleeping baby,

135
00:04:46,544 --> 00:04:46,975
honey.

136
00:04:47,155 --> 00:04:49,222
Touch it as little as you can.

137
00:04:49,572 --> 00:04:51,593
Turn the dough out on a floured

138
00:04:51,593 --> 00:04:53,875
counter and pat it into a rectangle

139
00:04:53,875 --> 00:04:55,179
about an inch thick.

140
00:04:55,359 --> 00:04:57,358
Then fold it in thirds,

141
00:04:57,358 --> 00:04:58,575
like a letter.

142
00:04:58,755 --> 00:04:59,939
Pat it out again.

143
00:05:00,119 --> 00:05:01,605
Fold it again.

144
00:05:01,785 --> 00:05:03,539
Do that three times.

145
00:05:03,719 --> 00:05:06,434
That's how you get those layers you

146
00:05:06,434 --> 00:05:07,598
can peel apart,

147
00:05:07,598 --> 00:05:08,452
one by one,

148
00:05:08,452 --> 00:05:09,849
with your fingers.

149
00:05:10,029 --> 00:05:12,020
Then pat it to about an inch

150
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:13,442
thick one more time.

151
00:05:13,622 --> 00:05:15,388
In a minute I'll tell you how

152
00:05:15,388 --> 00:05:16,118
to cut them.

153
00:05:16,468 --> 00:05:19,521
Mistake number three is twisting the cutter.

154
00:05:19,701 --> 00:05:21,744
Almost everybody does it.

155
00:05:21,924 --> 00:05:23,580
You press the cutter down,

156
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:25,428
and then you give it a little

157
00:05:25,428 --> 00:05:26,510
twist to free it.

158
00:05:26,690 --> 00:05:29,338
But that twist seals the edges of

159
00:05:29,338 --> 00:05:30,703
the biscuit shut,

160
00:05:30,703 --> 00:05:32,147
and it can't rise.

161
00:05:32,327 --> 00:05:33,862
Press straight down,

162
00:05:33,862 --> 00:05:35,473
and lift straight up.

163
00:05:35,653 --> 00:05:36,977
No twisting.

164
00:05:37,157 --> 00:05:39,177
And put them on the baking sheet

165
00:05:39,177 --> 00:05:40,440
touching each other,

166
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:42,207
honey, shoulder to shoulder.

167
00:05:42,387 --> 00:05:44,731
They hold each other up and rise

168
00:05:44,731 --> 00:05:45,243
taller.

169
00:05:45,594 --> 00:05:48,155
Brush the tops with a little melted

170
00:05:48,155 --> 00:05:49,692
butter or buttermilk.

171
00:05:49,872 --> 00:05:53,002
Bake them at four hundred twenty-five degrees

172
00:05:53,002 --> 00:05:54,810
for about fifteen minutes,

173
00:05:54,810 --> 00:05:57,454
until they're tall and golden brown on

174
00:05:57,454 --> 00:05:57,732
top.

175
00:05:57,912 --> 00:05:59,095
While they bake,

176
00:05:59,095 --> 00:06:00,501
you make the gravy.

177
00:06:00,681 --> 00:06:03,620
It takes exactly the same amount of

178
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:04,628
time, honey.

179
00:06:04,808 --> 00:06:07,955
That's why this breakfast works so well.

180
00:06:08,135 --> 00:06:10,999
Everything comes out hot at the same

181
00:06:10,999 --> 00:06:11,397
time.

182
00:06:11,747 --> 00:06:13,707
So far, frozen butter,

183
00:06:13,707 --> 00:06:16,915
gentle hands, fold it like a letter,

184
00:06:16,915 --> 00:06:18,341
and no twisting.

185
00:06:18,521 --> 00:06:19,868
Now the gravy.

186
00:06:20,048 --> 00:06:23,362
Mistake number four is draining the sausage

187
00:06:23,362 --> 00:06:23,670
fat.

188
00:06:23,850 --> 00:06:26,496
Brown the sausage in a big skillet

189
00:06:26,496 --> 00:06:27,819
over medium heat,

190
00:06:27,819 --> 00:06:30,154
breaking it into small pieces,

191
00:06:30,154 --> 00:06:32,256
until there's no pink left.

192
00:06:32,436 --> 00:06:34,854
And leave every drop of that fat

193
00:06:34,854 --> 00:06:35,686
in the pan,

194
00:06:35,686 --> 00:06:36,139
honey.

195
00:06:36,319 --> 00:06:38,177
That fat is your gravy.

196
00:06:38,357 --> 00:06:41,243
Without it, the flour has nothing to

197
00:06:41,243 --> 00:06:43,407
cook in, and you get paste.

198
00:06:43,758 --> 00:06:46,672
Sprinkle the quarter cup of flour right

199
00:06:46,672 --> 00:06:49,063
over the sausage and stir it in.

200
00:06:49,243 --> 00:06:50,581
And now cook it,

201
00:06:50,581 --> 00:06:53,341
stirring, for a full two minutes,

202
00:06:53,341 --> 00:06:56,685
until the flour smells toasty and nutty.

203
00:06:56,865 --> 00:06:58,936
That's mistake number five,

204
00:06:58,936 --> 00:06:59,779
sweetheart.

205
00:06:59,959 --> 00:07:01,341
Raw flour.

206
00:07:01,521 --> 00:07:04,061
People add the milk right away,

207
00:07:04,061 --> 00:07:07,338
and the gravy tastes like library paste.

208
00:07:07,518 --> 00:07:08,899
Two whole minutes.

209
00:07:09,079 --> 00:07:10,020
Count them.

210
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:11,928
Then pour in the milk,

211
00:07:11,928 --> 00:07:14,050
about a half cup at a time,

212
00:07:14,050 --> 00:07:15,621
stirring constantly.

213
00:07:15,972 --> 00:07:18,888
Keep stirring and adding milk as it

214
00:07:18,888 --> 00:07:20,221
thickens, honey.

215
00:07:20,401 --> 00:07:22,479
It looks too thin at first.

216
00:07:22,659 --> 00:07:23,890
Don't panic.

217
00:07:24,070 --> 00:07:26,252
It thickens as it simmers.

218
00:07:26,432 --> 00:07:28,990
Let it bubble gently for about five

219
00:07:28,990 --> 00:07:31,548
minutes, until it coats the back of

220
00:07:31,548 --> 00:07:32,133
a spoon.

221
00:07:32,313 --> 00:07:34,671
Add the salt and all that black

222
00:07:34,671 --> 00:07:35,204
pepper.

223
00:07:35,384 --> 00:07:38,066
Sausage gravy should be peppery.

224
00:07:38,246 --> 00:07:40,530
If it gets too thick sitting on

225
00:07:40,530 --> 00:07:43,035
the stove, just splash in a little

226
00:07:43,035 --> 00:07:43,772
more milk.

227
00:07:43,952 --> 00:07:44,947
Stay with me,

228
00:07:44,947 --> 00:07:47,168
because now comes the secret.

229
00:07:47,518 --> 00:07:49,435
Here's my mother's secret,

230
00:07:49,435 --> 00:07:50,246
sweetheart.

231
00:07:50,426 --> 00:07:53,013
She didn't use a biscuit cutter at

232
00:07:53,013 --> 00:07:53,317
all.

233
00:07:53,497 --> 00:07:55,273
We couldn't afford one.

234
00:07:55,453 --> 00:07:57,804
She used a plain drinking glass,

235
00:07:57,804 --> 00:07:58,980
the jelly glass,

236
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:00,155
dipped in flour.

237
00:08:00,335 --> 00:08:02,437
But the trick wasn't the glass.

238
00:08:02,617 --> 00:08:05,090
It was what she did right before.

239
00:08:05,270 --> 00:08:07,001
She put the cut biscuits,

240
00:08:07,001 --> 00:08:08,524
on their baking sheet,

241
00:08:08,524 --> 00:08:11,432
into the icebox for fifteen minutes before

242
00:08:11,432 --> 00:08:12,955
they went in the oven.

243
00:08:13,135 --> 00:08:15,504
Cold biscuits into a hot oven.

244
00:08:15,854 --> 00:08:18,614
Those fifteen minutes in the cold firm

245
00:08:18,614 --> 00:08:20,067
up the butter again,

246
00:08:20,067 --> 00:08:23,045
after all your patting and folding warmed

247
00:08:23,045 --> 00:08:23,481
it up.

248
00:08:23,661 --> 00:08:26,184
So when they hit that hot oven,

249
00:08:26,184 --> 00:08:28,870
the steam bursts out all at once,

250
00:08:28,870 --> 00:08:31,068
and they shoot straight up,

251
00:08:31,068 --> 00:08:31,556
honey.

252
00:08:31,736 --> 00:08:33,060
Twice as tall.

253
00:08:33,240 --> 00:08:36,479
That's what my father called skyscrapers.

254
00:08:36,659 --> 00:08:38,188
And at the very end,

255
00:08:38,188 --> 00:08:40,864
she stirred a tiny pinch of cayenne

256
00:08:40,864 --> 00:08:42,011
into the gravy.

257
00:08:42,191 --> 00:08:43,909
You don't taste the heat.

258
00:08:44,089 --> 00:08:46,272
It just makes the pepper sing.

259
00:08:46,622 --> 00:08:48,724
And if you want to make them

260
00:08:48,724 --> 00:08:51,428
ahead, sweetheart, here's what I do.

261
00:08:51,608 --> 00:08:52,899
Cut the biscuits,

262
00:08:52,899 --> 00:08:54,721
put them on a sheet pan,

263
00:08:54,721 --> 00:08:56,391
and freeze them solid.

264
00:08:56,571 --> 00:08:58,754
Then put them in a freezer bag.

265
00:08:58,934 --> 00:09:00,196
On Saturday morning,

266
00:09:00,196 --> 00:09:02,656
put them straight from the freezer into

267
00:09:02,656 --> 00:09:03,477
the hot oven,

268
00:09:03,477 --> 00:09:05,874
no thawing, and add about five minutes

269
00:09:05,874 --> 00:09:07,073
to the baking time.

270
00:09:07,253 --> 00:09:10,565
Frozen biscuits rise even taller than chilled

271
00:09:10,565 --> 00:09:10,933
ones.

272
00:09:11,113 --> 00:09:12,794
Keep a bag in your freezer,

273
00:09:12,794 --> 00:09:15,346
and you're never more than twenty minutes

274
00:09:15,346 --> 00:09:16,279
from breakfast.

275
00:09:16,630 --> 00:09:19,905
Split the biscuits open while they're hot,

276
00:09:19,905 --> 00:09:21,777
two halves on the plate,

277
00:09:21,777 --> 00:09:22,713
cut side up.

278
00:09:22,893 --> 00:09:25,894
Ladle that gravy right over the top,

279
00:09:25,894 --> 00:09:27,978
all the way to the edges.

280
00:09:28,158 --> 00:09:30,217
A fried egg on the side if

281
00:09:30,217 --> 00:09:32,117
it's a special Saturday.

282
00:09:32,297 --> 00:09:34,306
My father ate four.

283
00:09:34,486 --> 00:09:37,636
Danny ate three and pretended he'd only

284
00:09:37,636 --> 00:09:38,282
had two.

285
00:09:38,462 --> 00:09:41,138
And any leftover biscuits got split,

286
00:09:41,138 --> 00:09:42,476
toasted in butter,

287
00:09:42,476 --> 00:09:45,449
and spread with my mother's apple butter

288
00:09:45,449 --> 00:09:47,158
for an afternoon snack.

289
00:09:47,509 --> 00:09:49,158
When I married Harold,

290
00:09:49,158 --> 00:09:51,781
the very first breakfast I made him

291
00:09:51,781 --> 00:09:53,505
was biscuits and gravy,

292
00:09:53,505 --> 00:09:55,678
with my mother's jelly glass,

293
00:09:55,678 --> 00:09:57,927
which she gave me as a wedding

294
00:09:57,927 --> 00:09:58,526
present.

295
00:09:58,706 --> 00:10:00,084
He ate five,

296
00:10:00,084 --> 00:10:00,773
honey.

297
00:10:00,953 --> 00:10:01,650
Five.

298
00:10:01,830 --> 00:10:02,882
And he said,

299
00:10:02,882 --> 00:10:05,162
Ruthie, I'm never leaving.

300
00:10:05,342 --> 00:10:06,630
And he didn't.

301
00:10:06,810 --> 00:10:08,424
Forty-five years.

302
00:10:08,604 --> 00:10:11,228
I still have that jelly glass.

303
00:10:11,408 --> 00:10:13,417
It's in my cupboard right now.

304
00:10:13,597 --> 00:10:16,000
I use it every single time.

305
00:10:16,350 --> 00:10:17,945
Make it this Saturday,

306
00:10:17,945 --> 00:10:21,063
sweetheart, and chill those biscuits first.

307
00:10:21,243 --> 00:10:23,077
Tell me in the comments,

308
00:10:23,077 --> 00:10:25,827
what did your family eat on Saturday

309
00:10:25,827 --> 00:10:26,514
mornings?

310
00:10:26,694 --> 00:10:29,288
There are more of my everyday recipes

311
00:10:29,288 --> 00:10:31,741
in the playlist right there on your

312
00:10:31,741 --> 00:10:32,232
screen.

313
00:10:32,412 --> 00:10:34,154
Good night, honey.
